WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.637 --> 00:00:03.703
-♪ Hey, hey, hey, robot ♪

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:05.437
♪ Hey, robot ♪

00:00:05.437 --> 00:00:06.973
-Now, I'm gonna need some help.

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.608
So joining me now is the star

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:11.845
of the new Hulu show
"Nine Perfect Strangers."

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:14.547
Please welcome Melissa McCarthy!

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.749
What are you talking about? Yes!

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.218
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:19.252
-Hey!

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.653
-Hi, bud.

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:24.390
-When you think
of technology and robots,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.491
you think of me, right?

00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:26.792
-Yeah, exactly correct.

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:28.695
Yeah, now, I think
this is going to be perfect,

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:30.996
'cause here's why
I did think about you.

00:00:30.996 --> 00:00:34.333
Because this is based on a real
board game called "Hey Robot"

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.769
that you can play at home.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:37.503
It's like the classic game
"Password"

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:39.572
but your partner
is a smart speaker.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.442
First, I'll pick a card
with a word on it.

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:44.177
Then we'll take turns
asking a question

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:47.447
to get the speaker to say that
word somewhere in its answer.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:50.183
If it does, you get a point.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:52.651
And, Melissa, the reason
why I did think of you --

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:55.788
because you're actually
one of the new voices of Alexa.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.724
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.158
-It's true,

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:01.727
which is super creepy
in my house.

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:04.964
My kids, I think,
are very rattled by it.

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.266
And Ben likes to talk
to it a lot. But, yeah,

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:11.037
I guess they really needed,
like, a nasal Chicago voice.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.438
-[ Laughs ] Well, they got --

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.941
This just must be --
It's amazing.

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:17.209
Alright, well, so, instead of
Alexa, we're going to use --

00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:18.711
We're gonna be
asking you instead.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:21.847
Well, not you. But we'll ask
the Alexa you, okay?

00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:23.315
-Great.
-Alright. I will go first.

00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:24.817
Here we go.
-Okay.

00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:27.987
-Alright, I have to
get it to say chameleon.

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:30.756
I guess I'm not hiding that
from this.

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:33.893
Alright, ready? I have to get it
to say that. Okay.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:38.864
Hey, Melissa, what are the three
biggest hits of Culture Club?

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:41.034
-Here's something
I found on the Web.

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:45.804
According to access.com,
between late '83 and early 1984,

00:01:45.804 --> 00:01:47.807
Culture Club was the biggest
band in new wave

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:49.943
as "Color by Numbers"
spawned two more smash hits,

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:52.578
and "Miss me Blind," #5 Hot 100,

00:01:52.578 --> 00:01:54.547
and "It's a Miracle," #4 U.K.,

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.516
#13, Hot 100.

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:57.817
-Yeah, okay.

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:58.884
What?!
[ Buzzer ]

00:01:58.884 --> 00:02:01.454
"Miss Me Blind"? Okay.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.455
You're laughing?
You think that's funny?

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:05.225
That was so bizarre
to hear your voice.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:06.693
-"She's not gonna shut up."

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:08.127
[ Laughter ]

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.663
-Alright. They didn't get it.
Your chance to steal.

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:11.964
Can you get --
-Ooh.

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:14.099
-Can you get you get you
to say this word?

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:15.535
-Here's something creepy.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:20.306
Hey, Melissa, what do you call
a lizard that changes color?

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.508
-Here's something I found
on the Web.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:25.645
According to
walkhighlands.co.uk,

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.446
common lizard
is the more usual name,

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:30.182
but it retains its other moniker
via it's scientific name,

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:31.918
zootoca vivipara.

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:32.951
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.951 --> 00:02:34.353
-Ah.
[ Buzzer ]

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:35.988
Ah, see, if the word was --
-You know what's it's weird

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:37.390
is they don't give me
any of that stuff.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.725
I just know it
off the top of my head, so...

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:42.061
-Wait, you know
"zacoca babaveba"?

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.696
Alright, we're going to move on.

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:45.298
That one -- Well, no one
gets a point for that one.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:49.101
Melissa, it's your chance.
You're up.

00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:53.106
You have to get you, your Alexa,
to say...

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:56.376
meditation.

00:02:56.376 --> 00:03:00.312
-Hey, Melissa,
when you go to a spa retreat

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:04.283
and you sit quietly,
what is that called?

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:06.218
-I don't have an answer
for that.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:07.887
[ Laughter ]
-Why don't you try --

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:11.657
Why don't you do it again?
No, do it again. Ask it again.

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:13.426
-Okay, ready.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:16.663
Hey, Melissa,
can you say meditation?

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:18.564
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:20.566
-The states of meditation...

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.035
[ Bell dinging ]
-Yeah, you got that one.

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:24.236
We got to clarify the rules.

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:25.404
-You didn't say
we couldn't cheat.

00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:26.972
-That's not in the rules.
That's --

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:30.443
[ Soothing music playing ]

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:33.045
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:36.749
♪♪

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:39.585
-This feels good.
-This feels great.

00:03:39.585 --> 00:03:41.754
This feels great.
This must be the most --

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:43.522
the most personal interview
we've ever had,

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:46.125
the deepest, by far.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:47.727
Wow. Hey, Melissa, stop.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.229
I didn't know you could do that.

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:51.964
Alright, Melissa,
this is it right here.

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:53.599
This -- It's 1-0,

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:55.734
and it's your turn
since you are our guest.

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:57.103
This is the final round.

00:03:57.103 --> 00:04:00.239
This is worth 1,000 points
right here.

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:05.044
Can you get your version
of Alexa to say...

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.846
jerk?

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:13.853
-I don't know what year.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:16.655
Hey, Alexa,

00:04:16.655 --> 00:04:19.391
name a Steve Martin film

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:22.728
that also could be
considered an insult.

00:04:22.728 --> 00:04:24.330
-Hmm, I don't know that.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.898
[ Audience groans ]

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:27.767
-I was going to
go with that, as well.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:30.803
Uh -- Uh --

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:32.138
-How about -- How about,

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:37.175
"Melissa, what was
Steve Martin's breakout film?"

00:04:37.175 --> 00:04:39.312
-Tariq's playing now.

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.280
-I didn't know
we could ask for help.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.216
-Yeah, yeah, I guess --

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:48.154
Hey, Melissa, what was
Steve Martin's breakout film?

00:04:50.522 --> 00:04:53.392
-Steve Martin's first film
was "Father of the Bride."

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:54.760
-What?!

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.496
-That's not true!

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:58.664
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:01.267
-Dude, what is --
That's not true.

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.935
Alright, I got one. I got one.
-That's not true.

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:05.037
-Here we go.
That is not correct at all.

00:05:05.037 --> 00:05:07.306
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:08.774
Hey --
-Shame on you, Melissa.

00:05:08.774 --> 00:05:13.980
-Hey, Melissa, what's a popular
type of chicken in Jamaica?

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.148
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:18.417
-Hmm, I'm just not sure.
Apologies.

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:20.953
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:22.722
[ Buzzer ]
-Maybe you got to travel more.

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:25.058
[ Laughter ]

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:31.497
Hey, Melissa, name the comedians
that are famous for prank calls.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:33.466
-[ Chuckles ]

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:36.135
-Here's something
I found on reference.com.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.536
Celebrities who were famous

00:05:37.536 --> 00:05:39.405
primarily for making
prank phone calls

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:42.808
include the Tube Bar Pranksters,
John Elmo and Jim Davidson,

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:43.842
and the Jerky Boys.

00:05:43.842 --> 00:05:45.745
-Yes! That's right.
That's right.

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:48.581
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:48.581 --> 00:05:50.850
-What?!

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.585
-Melissa McCarthy, everybody!

